istently shows Pingree ahead by 8-13 points, including a Republican-sponsored survey", "Expert ratings and prediction markets uniformly favor the Democrat", "Pingree holds a clear fundraising and cash-on-hand edge over Charles", "Open seat and independent Rick Bennett's campaign add mild uncertainty"], "rationale": "Every recent poll puts Pingree ahead by high single digits or more, expert ratings and markets all point Democratic, and she holds a clear money advantage; the open seat and a credible independent keep this short of a total lock, but the lead is wide and stable.", "watch": "A consolidated moderate vote behind Bennett or a late surge by Charles off the debate coverage could tighten the race.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Janet Mills (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Maine's governor's race is an open contest now that Janet Mills is term-limited, with Democrat Hannah Pingree facing Republican Bobby Charles and independent Rick Bennett. Polling through late September consistently shows Pingree ahead, with margins ranging from eight to thirteen points across pollsters, including one with Republican sponsorship. The race has centered on national issues and state spending, with the candidates clashing in the first televised debate in early October.\n\nRaters overwhelmingly place the race on the Democratic side, with most calling it solid and the rest likely Democratic. Prediction markets price Pingree's chances in the mid-nineties. Pingree also holds a clear fundraising and cash-on-hand edge, and Senator Angus King's endorsement adds establishment weight. Bennett's independent run is a mild wildcard, but nothing in recent coverage suggests the race is narrowing.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9757, "margin": 10.9}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9716, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9705, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9685, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.96